  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe current best solution achieves a bound of 0.38085, and our goal is to improve it to 0.380876 or lower. The existing approach uses local optimization with random restarts and grid refinement, which can get stuck in local optima. Our approach combines global optimization with local refinement using a multi-resolution strategy. We start with the parent solution (n=47) and refine it to finer grids (n=94, 188, 376) using interpolation and repair mechanisms. The key improvements are:\n1. Using a global optimizer (CMA-ES) for exploration on coarse grids\n2. Using local refinement (trust-constrained) for polishing on finer grids\n3. Incorporating a repair function that maintains both integral constraint and bounds\n4. Allocating decreasing budgets per grid size to stay within the 1100-second limit\n\nThe approach addresses the bottleneck of local optimization by leveraging grid refinement and hybrid methods. The repair mechanism ensures feasibility while preserving the integral constraint. We use a time budget allocation strategy to maximize evaluations while minimizing runtime.\n\nMathematical specification:\nThe problem is to minimize C5 = max_k \u222b_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx subject to:\n- h(x) \u2208 [0,1] for all x\n- \u222b_0^2 h(x)dx = 1\n\nDiscrete representation:\n- Represent h as n-point samples on [0,2] with dx=2/n\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i, and \u2211h[i] = n/2\n- Objective: max_k (np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full')*dx)\n\nOptimization formulation:\n- Variables: h \u2208 \u211d^n with box constraints [0,1]^n and equality constraint \u2211h[i] = n/2\n- Objective: Non-convex, defined via correlation\n- Repair: Clip values to [0,1] and adjust sum uniformly\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   - Set random seed and retrieve initial_h_values if provided\n   - Initialize best_h, best_c5, and time tracking\n   - Define repair_vector and upsample_vector functions\n   - Define n_values and time_budgets\n\n2. Grid loop:\n   - For each grid size n in n_values:\n     - If time_used \u2265 budget_s, break\n     - Generate multiple restarts (5 per grid)\n     - For each restart:\n       - Repair initial guess to satisfy constraints\n       - Set method and options (CMA-ES for n\u2264100, SLSQP otherwise)\n       - Define objective and constraint functions\n       - Run optimizer with time tracking\n       - Repair solution and evaluate objective\n       - Update best solution if improved\n\n3. Repair function:\n   - Clip values to [0,1]\n   - Adjust sum to n/2 by distributing delta uniformly\n   - Re-clip to maintain bounds\n\n4. Upsample function:\n   - Perform linear interpolation from coarse to fine grid\n   - Repair the interpolated solution\n\n5. Objective function:\n   - Compute full correlation between h and 1-h\n   - Return max correlation * dx\n\n6. Time budget:\n   - Allocate decreasing budgets per grid size (500s, 300s, 250s, 50s)\n   - Track time per restart and break if budget exceeded\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n- Grid sizes: [47, 94, 188, 376]\n- Time budgets: [500, 300, 250, 50] seconds per grid\n- Restart strategy: 5 restarts per grid, parent as first guess if available\n- Repair: Clip values and adjust sum uniformly\n- Solvers: CMA-ES for n\u2264100, SLSQP otherwise\n\nComputebudget and robustness:\n- Dominant costs: Correlation computation (O(n^2)) and optimization\n- Estimates: n=47: ~100s per restart, n=94: ~60s, n=188: ~50s, n=376: ~10s\n- Budget allocation: 500s for n=47, 300s for n=94, 250s for n=188, 50s for n=376\n- Robustness: Repair ensures feasibility, fallback to parent if no improvement\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Feasibility check: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u2211h[i] = n/2\n- Objective consistency: Re-evaluate best candidate using np.correlate\n- Output: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) where h_values is the best solution array",
